hadiths[263]{hadithnumber,text}:
"18305","Abū al-Zaʿrāʾ reported that the Dajjāl was mentioned before ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, who said people will split into three groups: a group that follows him, a group that joins the land of their forefathers where shīḥ grows, and a group that holds the bank of this Euphrates, fighting him while he fights them, until the believers gather in western Syria and send a vanguard including a horseman on a reddish or piebald horse, and they are killed without returning with news. ʿAbdullāh added that people claim the Messiah will descend and kill him, then Yaʾjūj and Maʾjūj will emerge and spread corruption, then Allāh will send a worm-like creature into their ears and nostrils so they die and the earth stinks, then the people will supplicate, Allāh will send water to purify the earth, then a cold freezing wind that takes every believer, then the Hour will fall upon the wicked, then the angel will blow the Trumpet between heaven and earth so all die except whom Allāh wills, then after an interval Allāh will send water from beneath the Throne like the fluid of men by which bodies and flesh grow as earth grows with rain, then the angel will blow again so every soul returns to its body and they stand as one man before the Lord of the worlds. Then Allāh will present Himself to creation, and every people that worshipped other than Allāh will follow what it worshipped: the Jews will say they worshipped ʿUzayr, the Christians will say they worshipped the Messiah, and they will be shown Hell as a mirage and as drink, until the Muslims remain and affirm they worship Allāh alone without partner, then the Shin will be uncovered so every believer falls prostrate while the hypocrites remain with backs as one slab as though fitted with skewers, then the Bridge will be laid over Hell and people will cross by deeds like lightning, wind, birds, and beasts, until the last man crawls on his belly and blames his deeds, then intercession will be permitted beginning with Jibrīl, then Ibrāhīm, then Mūsā or ʿĪsā, peace be upon them, then your Prophet ﷺ will intercede with the Praiseworthy Station, then angels, prophets, martyrs, righteous and believers will intercede, then the Most Merciful of the merciful will bring out of the Fire more than all creation brought out, leaving none with any good therein. > **""People will divide into three groups, the believers will gather in western Syria, every soul will return to its body, and your Prophet ﷺ will intercede with the Praiseworthy Station.""**"
"18306","Abū Murayyah, or ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported doubtfully from the Prophet ﷺ that the two blowers are in the second heaven, the head of one in the west and his feet in the east, or the reverse, waiting for when they are commanded to blow the Trumpet, and then they will blow. > **""The two blowers wait in the second heaven for when they are commanded to blow the Trumpet.""**"
"18307","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he could not live at ease while the bearer of the Horn had seized it, bent his forehead, and inclined his hearing waiting for the command, so the Companions found it grievous and the Prophet ﷺ told them to say that Allāh suffices them and He is the best Disposer. > **""How can I enjoy while the bearer of the Horn has seized it and listens for the command? Say: Allāh suffices us and He is the best Disposer.""**"
"18308","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, commenting on the verse when the Trumpet is sounded, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he could not live at ease while the bearer of the Horn had seized it and bent his forehead waiting to blow, so the Companions asked what to say and he told them to say that Allāh suffices them, He is the best Disposer, and upon Allāh they rely. > **""How can I enjoy while the bearer of the Horn waits to blow? Say: Allāh suffices us and He is the best Disposer, upon Allāh we rely.""**"
"18309","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said every morning two angels call glory to the Sovereign, the Holy, two angels pray for the spender and against the withholder, two angels entrusted with the Trumpet wait for the command to blow, two angels call the seeker of good to advance and the seeker of evil to desist, and two angels warn of trial between men and women. > **""Two angels entrusted with the Trumpet wait for when they are commanded to blow.""**"
"18310","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith reported that he was with ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, when Kaʿb al-Ḥabr mentioned Isrāfīl, so she asked Kaʿb to describe him, and he said he has four wings, two in the air, one wrapped about him, one upon his shoulder, with the Pen upon his ear that writes when revelation descends while the angels study, and the angel of the Trumpet kneels on one knee with the other raised, holding the Trumpet with bent back, commanded to blow when he sees Isrāfīl draw his wing together. ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, confirmed she heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say the same. > **""The angel of the Trumpet holds it with bent back, commanded to blow when Isrāfīl draws his wing.""**"
"18311","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said a black cloud like a shield will rise from the west before the Hour, spread until it fills the sky, then a caller will proclaim that the command of Allāh has come so do not seek to hasten it. The Prophet ﷺ swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that two men spreading a garment will not fold it, a man plastering his basin will never drink from it, and a man milking his she-camel will never drink it. > **""The command of Allāh has come, so do not seek to hasten it; two men spreading a garment will not fold it.""**"
"18312","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the Hour will surely come while two men have a garment between them that they neither fold nor trade, while a man has raised his morsel to his mouth without tasting it, and while a man plasters his basin without drinking from it. > **""The Hour will come while two men have a garment between them that they neither fold nor trade.""**"
"18313","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the earth, meaning thereby the land, will surely jolt you with the kicking of a young camel. > **""The earth will surely jolt you with the kicking of a young camel.""**"
"18314","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that dust eats all of man except the tailbone. When asked of its likeness, he said it is like a mustard seed, from which you will grow. > **""Dust eats all of man except the tailbone, like a mustard seed from which you will grow.""**"
"18315","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the people of Lūt were destroyed only at the time of adhān, and the Hour will come only at the time of adhān, understood to mean the dawn adhān, the time of seeking forgiveness and supplication. > **""The people of Lūt were destroyed at the adhān, and the Hour will come at the adhān.""**"
"18316","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say that the Muslims will be raised on the Day of Resurrection upon their intentions. > **""The Muslims will be raised on the Day of Resurrection upon their intentions.""**"
"18317","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said every soul will be gathered upon its desire, so whoever desired disbelief will be with the disbelievers and his deeds will not benefit him at all. > **""Every soul will be gathered upon its desire, and whoever desired disbelief will be with the disbelievers.""**"
"18318","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said you will be gathered barefoot, naked, and uncircumcised. > **""You will be gathered barefoot, naked, and uncircumcised.""**"
"18319","Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that people will be gathered on the Day of Resurrection walking, barefoot, and uncircumcised. When asked whether men would look at women, he said every person on that Day will have a concern to occupy him. > **""People will be gathered walking, barefoot, uncircumcised, and every person will have a concern to occupy him.""**"
"18320","Umm Salamah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that she heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say people will be gathered barefoot and naked, so she asked how some could look at others, and he said people will be preoccupied. When asked by what, he said by the unrolling of scrolls containing even an ant's weight and a mustard seed's weight. > **""People will be gathered barefoot and naked, preoccupied by the unrolled scrolls.""**"
"18321","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said people will be gathered barefoot and naked, so a woman asked how some could look at others, and he said eyes will be fixed aloft, raising his own gaze to the sky. She asked him to pray that Allāh cover her nakedness, so he prayed for her covering. > **""Eyes will be fixed aloft; O Allāh, cover her nakedness.""**"
"18322","Sawdah bint Zamʿah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said people will be raised barefoot, naked, and uncircumcised, with sweat bridling them and reaching the earlobes. When she asked whether some would see others, he said people will be preoccupied, every person having a concern to occupy him. > **""People will be raised barefoot, naked, uncircumcised, bridled by sweat, and every person will have a concern to occupy him.""**"
"18323","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said people will be gathered on mounts to reach the gathering, Ṣāliḥ upon his she-camel, his two sons al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn upon his she-camel al-ʿAḍbāʾ, himself upon al-Burāq whose stride reaches its farthest sight, and Bilāl upon a she-camel of Paradise calling the adhān purely and the testimony truly, until when he says that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allāh the believers of former and latter peoples testify, accepted from some and rejected from others. > **""People will be gathered upon mounts to reach the gathering, and I shall be upon al-Burāq.""**"
"18324","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said there is no loneliness for the people of Lā ilāha illā Allāh in death, nor in graves, nor in resurrection, as though he sees them at the Blast shaking dust from their heads and saying praise belongs to Allāh who removed grief from them. > **""There is no loneliness for the people of Lā ilāha illā Allāh in death, graves, or resurrection.""**"
"18325","Sulaym ibn ʿĀmir al-Kalāʿī reported that they asked al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib al-Kindī, may Allāh be pleased with him, whether he had seen the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and he swore he had, that the Prophet ﷺ once took his earlobe while he walked with his uncle, then asked them to narrate what they heard. He said he heard the Prophet ﷺ say that all from the miscarried fetus to the aged elder will be gathered in the form of Ādam, with the heart of Ayyūb and the beauty of Yūsuf, peace be upon them, beardless and with kohl-lined eyes. When asked about the disbeliever, he said he will be enlarged for the Fire until his skin is forty cubits thick and his molar like Uḥud. > **""People will be gathered in the form of Ādam, with the heart of Ayyūb and the beauty of Yūsuf, while the disbeliever is enlarged for the Fire.""**"
"18326","Al-Miqdām ibn al-Aswad, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say people from the miscarried fetus to the aged elder will be gathered at thirty-three years of age, in the form of Ādam, with the beauty of Yūsuf and the heart of Ayyūb, peace be upon them, with kohl-lined eyes and full hair. > **""People will be gathered at thirty-three years, in the form of Ādam, with the beauty of Yūsuf and the heart of Ayyūb.""**"
"18327","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that Allāh will raise some people on the Day of Resurrection in the form of ants whom people trample with their feet, and it will be said these are the arrogant of the world. > **""Allāh will raise some in the form of ants; these are the arrogant of the world.""**"
"18328","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the arrogant will be gathered on the Day of Resurrection in the form of ants. > **""The arrogant will be gathered in the form of ants.""**"
"18329","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said if a man knew what comes to him after death, he would not eat a bite nor drink a draught except weeping and striking his chest. > **""If a man knew what comes after death, he would not eat nor drink except weeping.""**"
"18330","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said death compared to what follows it is but like the butting of a goat. > **""Death compared to what follows it is but like the butting of a goat.""**"
"18331","ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-ʿAṭṭār reported from Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, as a raised report, that man has met nothing since Allāh created him harder than death, yet death is easier than what follows it, and they will meet such terror on that Day that sweat will bridle them until ships could sail in it. > **""Man has met nothing harder than death, yet death is easier than what follows it.""**"
"18332","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said not to wish for death, for the terror of the ascent is severe, with the remainder of the report as already detailed in the book of repentance concerning long life. > **""Do not wish for death, for the terror of the ascent is severe.""**"
"18333","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said people will be raised on the Day of Resurrection while the sky drizzles over them. > **""People will be raised on the Day of Resurrection while the sky drizzles over them.""**"
"18334","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the sun will draw near on the Day of Resurrection to a mile's distance, its heat greatly increased so that creeping creatures boil as pots boil, and people will sweat according to their sins, some to the ankles, some to the shins, some to the waist, and some bridled by sweat. > **""The sun will draw near to a mile, and people will sweat according to their sins.""**"
"18335","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say the sun will draw near the earth and people will sweat, some to the heels, some to mid-shin, some to the knees, some to the buttocks, some to the waist, some to the shoulders, some to the neck, and some with sweat bridling the mouth, as the Prophet ﷺ gestured with his hand, while some will be wholly covered by sweat. > **""The sun will draw near and people will sweat, some bridled by sweat at the mouth.""**"
"18336","Saʿīd ibn ʿUmayr al-Anṣārī reported that he sat with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar and Abū Saʿīd, may Allāh be pleased with them, and one told the other that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ mention that sweat on the Day of Resurrection would reach the earlobe, while the other said it would bridle him, and Ibn ʿUmar gestured from earlobe to mouth as though both descriptions were one. > **""Sweat on the Day of Resurrection will reach the earlobe and bridle the mouth.""**"
"18337","Al-Miqdām, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the sun will draw near on the Day of Resurrection to a mile from people, its heat increased to scorch them, and they will stand in sweat according to deeds, some to the ankles, some to the knees, some to the waist, and some fully bridled, as the narrator saw the Prophet ﷺ gesture toward his mouth. > **""The sun will be a mile away, and people will stand in sweat according to their deeds.""**"
"18338","Muḥammad ibn Furāt reported that two men brought a dispute before Muḥārib, and when a witness testified against one, the accused affirmed the witness was known truthful yet had testified falsely against him. Muḥārib ibn Dithār warned him to fear Allāh, for he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, while admonishing a man, say the false witness's feet will not move until the Fire is assured for him, while on that Day birds will beat their wings and cast out their bellies' contents without any claim upon them. > **""The false witness's feet will not move until the Fire is assured for him.""**"
"18339","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said the whole earth will be Fire on the Day of Resurrection with Paradise beyond it, its maidens and cups visible, and by Him in Whose Hand is ʿAbdullāh's soul a man will pour sweat until his stature sinks in the earth, then it will rise to his nose, though reckoning has not yet touched him. Asked why, he said because of what people will witness. > **""The whole earth will be Fire, and a man will pour sweat to his nose before reckoning.""**"
"18340","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that there will be appointed for the disbeliever on the Day of Resurrection the measure of fifty thousand years as though he did nothing in the world, and the disbeliever will see Hell from forty years' journey and think he is falling into it. > **""Fifty thousand years will be appointed for the disbeliever, who will see Hell from forty years away.""**"
"18341","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that sweat will bridle a man on the Day of Resurrection until he says, my Lord, grant me relief even into the Fire. > **""Sweat will bridle a man until he says: My Lord, grant me relief even into the Fire.""**"
"18342","In a halted variant it is reported that the disbeliever will be so encompassed. > **""The disbeliever will be encompassed.""**"
"18343","In a variant in both collections he said the disbeliever will be reckoned on the Day of Resurrection until sweat bridles him. > **""The disbeliever will be reckoned until sweat bridles him.""**"
"18344","In another variant in al-Awsaṭ, the disbeliever will be bridled by his own sweat for the severity of that Day until he speaks out in anguish. > **""The disbeliever will be bridled by his sweat for the severity of that Day.""**"
"18345","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said sweat will cling to a man in the standing until he says, my Lord, Your sending me to the Fire is easier for me than what I suffer, though he knows the severity of its torment. > **""My Lord, Your sending me to the Fire is easier than what I suffer.""**"
"18346","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet of Allāh ﷺ said believers will be raised on the Day of Resurrection without body hair or beard, with kohl-lined eyes, at thirty years of age. > **""Believers will be raised beardless, with kohl-lined eyes, at thirty years.""**"
"18347","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that it was said, O Messenger of Allāh, how long is the Day whose measure is fifty thousand years, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said by Him in Whose Hand is his soul it will be lightened for the believer until it is lighter upon him than an ordained prayer he prayed in the world. > **""It will be lightened for the believer until lighter than an ordained prayer.""**"
"18348","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the Day when mankind stands before the Lord of the worlds that its measure of half a day of fifty thousand years will be eased for the believer like the sun's descent toward setting until it sets. > **""It will be eased for the believer like the sun's descent toward setting.""**"
"18349","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ to ask three things: how long people will stand before the Lord of the worlds, what will ease it for the believer, and whether there is a station between Paradise and Fire. He ﷺ said they will stand a thousand years without leave, the foremost believers will pass it like two men in long intimate converse then entering Paradise, and between Paradise and Fire lies his Basin, a month's length and width, whiter than milk and sweeter than honey, with silver cups and flasks, whoever drinks a cup never thirsting nor grieving until judgment is passed. > **""Between Paradise and Fire lies my Basin, a month's journey, whiter than milk and sweeter than honey.""**"
"18350","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that on the Day of Resurrection it will be said where are the poor and needy of this nation, and they will rise and be asked what they did. They will say, our Lord, we were tried and patient while authority was given to others, so Allāh will say they spoke truly and they will enter Paradise long before others while severe reckoning remains for rulers. Asked where believers will be then, he said pulpits of light will be set for them shaded by clouds, and that Day will be shorter upon the believers than an hour of daytime. > **""The poor will enter Paradise long before others, and that Day will be shorter upon the believers than an hour.""**"
"18351","Laqīṭ ibn ʿĀmir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he came as a delegate to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ with his companion Nahīk ibn ʿĀṣim after Rajab had passed, finding him after dawn prayer addressing the people that he had saved his voice four days to be heard, warning against distraction by self-talk, companions, or misguidance, and reminding that he will be asked whether he conveyed. When Laqīṭ asked what knowledge of the unseen he held, the Prophet ﷺ laughed, shook his head, and said the Lord reserved the five keys of the unseen known only to Allāh: knowledge of death, of the fluid in the womb, of tomorrow's provision, of the rain approaching the distressed while He laughs knowing relief is near, and of the Hour. Laqīṭ remarked they will never lack good from a Lord who laughs. He then asked for teaching for his people, and the Prophet ﷺ told him they would remain, then their Prophet ﷺ would pass, then the Blast would leave nothing alive upon the earth nor the angels with the Lord, then the Lord would traverse the earth emptied of lands, send rain from the Throne splitting every grave, raising men seated and confused as though yesterday alive, then ask how He will gather them scattered by winds, decay, and beasts, likening it to dead barren land revived by rain into one drink, and to seeing the sun and moon together without crowding, more able to see them and be seen, then presenting them with scrolls open hiding nothing, sprinkling water that leaves the Muslim's face like a white cloth and brands the disbeliever black as pitch, then the Prophet ﷺ withdrawing, the righteous following across a bridge over Fire where a coal makes one cry out, then reaching the Prophet's Basin most thirsty yet never seen, where every hand finds a cup purifying from filth, urine, and harm, while sun and moon are withheld. > **""Your Lord reserved the five keys of the unseen, and He will gather you as He gathers the earth's growth by rain.""**"
"18352","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that Allāh will gather the first and last for an appointed Day, standing forty years with eyes fixed skyward awaiting decisive judgment. Allāh will descend in shaded clouds from Throne to Footstool, and a caller will ask whether they accept their Lord's justice in consigning each people to what they worshipped in the world. Each party will go to sun, moon, stone idols, and likenesses, while for worshippers of ʿĪsā and ʿUzayr devils will be figured, leaving Muḥammad ﷺ and his nation. The Lord will come and ask why they do not go, and they will say they have a God not yet seen but known by a sign, the uncovering of the Shin, whereupon all who used to look will fall prostrate except those whose backs are like cows' horns unable to bow though once called sound. Then heads will be raised and light granted by deeds, some like a great mountain before them, some like a palm in the hand, down to the last whose light on his great toe shines and fades so he steps when lit and halts when quenched, with the Lord before them until crossing the Fire whose trace remains like a sword's edge. They will be told to pass according to light, some like an eye-blink, lightning, cloud, falling star, wind, horse's gallop, and camel's pace, until the least crawls on face, hands, and feet, sides struck by Fire, until saved to praise Allāh for deliverance after seeing it. He will be washed at a pool by Paradise's gate, restored to its fragrance and colour, see Paradise through the gate and beg entry, then beg a veil from the Fire's sound, then entering, see a dwelling ahead as though his present were a dream, repeatedly begging finer dwellings and swearing by Allāh's might to ask no more, receiving each, until silent, whereupon Allāh will ask why he asks no more, and he will answer he is abashed by asking and swearing, and Allāh will grant him his wish and more. > **""They will stand forty years awaiting judgment, then pass over the Fire according to their light.""**"
"18353","In a variant the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said people will stand before the Lord of the worlds forty years with eyes fixed, awaiting decisive judgment, then the like of the report of Zayd ibn Abī Unaysah was mentioned. > **""People will stand before the Lord of the worlds forty years awaiting decisive judgment.""**"
"18354","Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ used to tell them they will be gathered toward Bayt al-Maqdis, then assembled on the Day of Resurrection. > **""You will be gathered toward Bayt al-Maqdis, then assembled on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"18355","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, said whoever doubts the gathering is in Syria should recite the opening of Sūrat al-Ḥashr concerning those expelled for the first gathering, for the Prophet ﷺ said it, meaning Syria, is the land of gathering. > **""Syria is the land of the gathering.""**"
"18356","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said mankind will be gathered and a caller will ask whether it is justice to consign each people to what they worshipped, then their gods will be raised and followed until none remains but this nation. Asked why they linger, they will say they do not see their God whom they worshipped, whereupon He, blessed and exalted, will manifest Himself to them. Abū Burdah thrice swore by Allāh he heard Abū Mūsā narrate this from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. > **""Each people will follow what they worshipped, until only this nation remains before its Lord.""**"
"18357","Abū Mūsā, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said this nation will be gathered in three sorts on the Day of Resurrection: a sort entering Paradise without reckoning, a sort reckoned lightly then entering Paradise, and a sort coming with mountain-like burdens upon their carriers. Allāh will ask the angels, though knowing, who they are, and they will say servants who worshipped Him without partner, so He will say to lift the burdens from them, place them upon Jews and Christians, and enter Paradise by My mercy. > **""A sort will enter Paradise without reckoning, a sort after light reckoning, and burdens will be lifted from the third by mercy.""**"
"18358","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said you will be gathered on a single plain where the eye reaches you and the caller makes you hear. > **""You will be gathered on a single plain where the eye reaches you and the caller makes you hear.""**"
"18359","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that Allāh will gather the nations on the Day of Resurrection, then descend from His Throne to His Footstool, whose extent embraces the heavens and the earth. > **""Allāh will gather the nations, then descend from His Throne to His Footstool.""**"
"18360","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that there will come with him on the Day of Resurrection from his nation like the torrent and the night, crushing through people, so that the angels will say those who came with Muḥammad are more than came with all other nations and prophets. > **""Those who come with Muḥammad will be more than came with all other nations.""**"
"18361","Abū Dharr and Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he will surely recognise his nation among nations. Asked how, he said he will know them receiving their books in their right hands, known by the mark of prostration on their faces, and by their light streaming before them. > **""I will know them by their books in their right hands, the mark of prostration, and their light before them.""**"
"18362","In a variant, their light will stream before them and on their right hands. > **""Their light will stream before them and on their right hands.""**"
"18363","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he is the first permitted to prostrate on the Day of Resurrection and the first permitted to raise his head, looking before, behind, right, and left to recognise his nation among nations. Asked how among the nations from Nūḥ to his own, he said they are radiant with ablution marks, none else so, receiving books in right hands, with their offspring hastening before them. > **""I am the first permitted to prostrate and raise my head, and they are radiant with ablution marks.""**"
"18364","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said you are the radiant ones with ablution marks. > **""You are the radiant ones with ablution marks.""**"
"18365","ʿIkrimah reported that both Hands of Allāh are right, He will fold the heavens and seize them in His Hand saying, I am the Sovereign, where are the tyrants, where are the arrogant, then seize the earths in His other Hand saying the same. ʿUmar ibn Ḥamzah told this to Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh, who reported from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh will fold the heavens on the Day of Resurrection, seize them in His right Hand saying, I am the Sovereign, where are the tyrants, where are the arrogant, then fold the earths and seize them in His left, saying the same. > **""I am the Sovereign, where are the tyrants, where are the arrogant?""**"
"18366","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning Allāh's word when the earth is changed for another earth that it will be a white land upon which no blood was shed and no sin committed. > **""It will be a white land upon which no blood was shed and no sin committed.""**"
"18367","Al-Ḥasan reported that Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, narrated while in Madīnah that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said deeds will come on the Day of Resurrection: prayer will come saying, my Lord, I am prayer, and He will say it is upon good, then charity, then fasting, then all deeds likewise, until Islām comes saying, my Lord, You are Peace and I am Islām, so Allāh will say it is upon good, by it Today I seize and by it I grant, as Allāh said in His Book that whoever seeks other than Islām as religion it will never be accepted from him and he is among the losers in the Hereafter. > **""By Islām Today I seize and by it I grant; whoever seeks other than Islām it will never be accepted.""**"
"18368","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he saw the people gathered for the reckoning. > **""I saw the people gathered for the reckoning.""**"
"18369","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that word reached him of a report heard from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, so he bought a camel, saddled it, and travelled a month to Syria to ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays. Announcing himself at the door, Ibn Unays came out trailing his garment and they embraced. Jābir said he feared one of them would die before he heard the report on requital, so Ibn Unays said he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say Allāh will gather servants on the Day of Resurrection barefoot, naked, uncircumcised, with nothing, then call them with a voice heard by far and near: I am the Requiter, I am the Sovereign, none of Hell's people should enter Fire while owed a right by one of Paradise's people until it is requited, nor any of Paradise's people enter Paradise while one of Hell's people is owed a right by him until it is requited, even a slap. Asked how with nothing, he said by good and evil deeds. > **""I am the Requiter, I am the Sovereign; no right shall remain except it is requited, even a slap.""**"
"18370","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the feet of a servant will not move on the Day of Resurrection until he is asked about four: his life in what he spent it, his body in what he wore it out, his wealth in what he spent it and whence he earned it, and love of us, the Household. > **""The feet will not move until asked about life, body, wealth, and love of us, the Household.""**"
"18371","Abū Barzah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the feet will not move until asked about four: his body in what he wore it out, his life in what he spent it, his wealth whence earned and where spent, and love of us, the Household. When asked the sign of their love, he struck his hand upon the shoulder of ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him. > **""Feet will not move until asked about body, life, wealth, and love of us, the Household.""**"
"18372","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the feet of a servant will not move on the Day of Resurrection until he is asked about four: his youth in what he wore it out, his life in what he spent it, and his wealth whence he earned it and in what he spent it. > **""Feet will not move until asked about youth, life, and wealth whence earned and where spent.""**"
"18373","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the feet of a servant will not move on the Day of Resurrection until he is asked about four traits: his life in what he spent it, his youth in what he wore it out, his wealth whence he earned it and where he spent it, and his knowledge what he did with it. > **""Feet will not move until asked about life, youth, wealth, and what he did with knowledge.""**"
"18374","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say when the Day of Resurrection comes Allāh will summon a servant, halt him before Him, and ask him about his rank as He asks him about his wealth. > **""Allāh will halt a servant and ask him about his rank as about his wealth.""**"
"18375","Buraydah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said there is none among you but Allāh will speak to him with no veil nor interpreter between them. > **""Every one of you shall speak to Allāh with no veil nor interpreter.""**"
"18376","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUkaym reported that he heard ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, in this mosque begin with an oath before speaking: there is none among you but his Lord will be alone with him as one of you is alone with the full moon, saying, son of Ādam, what deceived you concerning Me, what answer did you give the messengers, what did you do, what did you do with what you knew, each repeated twice. > **""Son of Ādam, what deceived you concerning Me, what answer did you give the messengers, what did you do with what you knew?""**"
"18377","Thawbān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ magnified the matter of questioning, saying when the Day of Resurrection comes the people of Jāhiliyyah will arrive carrying their idols on their backs. Their Lord will ask them, and they will say, our Lord, no messenger was sent to us and no command reached us; had You sent one we would have been most obedient. He will ask whether they would obey if He commanded them, take their covenants, then command them to enter the Fire. They will go, fear upon seeing it, and return saying they fear and cannot enter, so He will say enter it debased. The Prophet of Allāh ﷺ said had they entered the first time it would have been coolness and safety upon them. > **""Had they entered the first time it would have been coolness and safety.""**"
"18378","Al-Ḥasan reported that Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, addressed them from the pulpit of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, saying he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say Allāh will present three excuses to Ādam, peace be upon him, on the Day of Resurrection. Allāh will say, O Ādam, had I not cursed liars, hated lying and oath-breaking, and threatened for it, I would have mercy today upon all your children for the torment prepared, but My word is true: if My messengers are belied and My command disobeyed I shall fill Hell with jinn and men together. And He will say, O Ādam, know I admit none of your offspring to Fire nor torment any with Fire except him whom I know would return to worse evil if returned to the world, unrepentant. And He will say, O Ādam, I have made you judge between Me and your offspring: stand by the Balance and see what is raised of their deeds; whoever's good outweighs evil by an atom's weight has Paradise, so you know I admit none to Fire but a wrongdoer. > **""I admit none of your offspring to Fire except him I know would return to worse evil.""**"
"18379","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that injustice is three kinds: an injustice Allāh will not forgive, an injustice He will forgive, and an injustice He will not leave. The unforgiven is shirk, for Allāh said shirk is a mighty wrong. The forgiven is servants' wronging themselves between them and their Lord. The unleft is servants' wronging one another until requital is taken among them. > **""Injustice is three kinds: shirk never forgiven, wrongs toward Allāh forgiven, and wrongs between servants never left until requited.""**"
"18380","Salmān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said there is a sin not forgiven, a sin not left, and a sin forgiven. The unforgiven is shirk with Allāh, the forgiven is the servant's sin between him and Allāh, and the unleft is servants' sins against one another. > **""There is a sin not forgiven, a sin not left, and a sin forgiven; shirk is not forgiven.""**"
"18381","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said there is a sin forgiven, a sin not forgiven, and a sin requited. The unforgiven is shirk with Allāh, the forgiven is your deed between you and your Lord, and the requited is your wronging your brother. > **""The unforgiven is shirk, the forgiven is between you and your Lord, and wronging your brother is requited.""**"
"18382","ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the registers with Allāh are three: one He disregards entirely, one He leaves nothing of, and one He does not forgive. The unforgiven is shirk, for Allāh said whoever associates with Allāh has Paradise forbidden to him. The disregarded is the servant's wronging himself between him and his Lord, such as abandoning a fast or prayer, which Allāh forgives and overlooks if He wills. The unleft is servants' wronging one another, whose requital is inevitable. > **""The registers are three: shirk never forgiven, wrongs toward Allāh overlooked, and wrongs between servants never left.""**"
"18383","Ḥudhayfah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that woe awaits the master on account of the slave, and woe awaits the slave on account of the master. > **""Woe to the master on account of the slave, and woe to the slave on account of the master.""**"
"18384","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said woe awaits the master on account of the slave and the slave on account of the master, the rich on account of the poor and the poor on account of the rich, the strong on account of the weak and the weak on account of the strong. > **""Woe to the rich on account of the poor, and to the strong on account of the weak, and the reverse.""**"
"18385","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said everything will dispute on the Day of Resurrection, even two goats over their butting. > **""Everything will dispute on the Day of Resurrection, even two goats over their butting.""**"
"18386","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said by Him in Whose Hand is his soul, all will dispute on the Day of Resurrection, even two goats over their butting. > **""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, even two goats will dispute over their butting.""**"
"18387","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the first two litigants on the Day of Resurrection will be neighbours. > **""The first two litigants on the Day of Resurrection will be neighbours.""**"
"18388","Abū Ayyūb, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the first to dispute on the Day of Resurrection will be a man and his wife, though her tongue will not speak; rather her hands and feet will testify against her for faults toward her husband, and his hands and feet for what he gave her. Then a man and his servants likewise, then market people, where no coins avail but the oppressor's good deeds go to the oppressed and the oppressed's evil deeds fall upon the oppressor. Then tyrants will be brought in iron shackles and ordered to the Fire, as Allāh said that all shall pass over it, then the God-fearing saved and wrongdoers left kneeling therein. > **""The first to dispute will be a man and his wife, then requital by deeds until tyrants are ordered to the Fire.""**"
"18389","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that Allāh will raise a servant without sin and ask whether he prefers requital by his deeds or by the blessing granted him. He will say, my Lord, You know I never disobeyed You, so a single blessing will be taken and consume all his good deeds, until he pleads for blessing and mercy, and Allāh will grant by blessing and mercy. Then a self-righteous servant seeing no sin will be asked whether he befriended Allāh's allies, and he will say he kept peace with all, and whether he opposed Allāh's enemies, and he will say none opposed him. So Allāh will say His mercy is not attained by one who befriends not His allies nor opposes His enemies. > **""A single blessing consumes all deeds, and My mercy is not attained without loyalty to My allies and enmity to My enemies.""**"
"18390","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that master and slave, husband and wife will all be brought and reckoned, until a man will be told he drank for pleasure on such a day, and a husband told he proposed to a woman with suitors and was married to her over them. > **""Master and slave, husband and wife will all be brought and reckoned.""**"
"18391","Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever is closely questioned in the reckoning perishes. > **""Whoever is closely questioned in the reckoning perishes.""**"
"18392","ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said none will be reckoned on the Day of Resurrection and then forgiven; rather the Muslim sees his deeds in his grave, as Allāh said that on that Day no man or jinn will be asked about his sin, and criminals will be known by their marks. > **""None will be reckoned then forgiven; criminals will be known by their marks.""**"
"18393","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said disgrace and exposure will weigh upon man on the Day of Resurrection until the servant wishes to be commanded into the Fire. > **""Disgrace will weigh upon man until he wishes to be commanded into the Fire.""**"
"18394","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, raised the report that an angel is entrusted with the Balance, and man will be halted between its pans. If his scale is heavy, an angel will call in a voice heard by all creation that so-and-so has attained bliss never followed by wretchedness, and if light, that so-and-so has met wretchedness never followed by bliss. > **""If his scale is heavy he has attained bliss never followed by wretchedness.""**"
"18395","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said sealed scrolls will be brought on the Day of Resurrection and set before Allāh, who will say to cast these and accept these. The angels will say, by Your might, we saw only good, and Allāh will say it was for other than His sake, and Today He accepts only what was sought for His sake. > **""Cast these and accept these; Today I accept only what was sought for My sake.""**"
"18396","In a variant the angels will say, by Your might, we wrote only what he did, and He will say they spoke truly, but his deed was for other than His sake. > **""We wrote only what he did, but his deed was for other than My sake.""**"
"18397","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said at the end of time his nation will be three groups: those worshipping Allāh sincerely, those worshipping for show, and those worshipping to devour people thereby. When Allāh gathers them He will ask each by His might and majesty what was intended by worship. The acquisitor will confess seeking people, and it will be said his gains avail nothing to refuge, take him to the Fire. The showy will confess seeking people's eyes, and it will be said nothing ascended thereof, take him to the Fire. The sincere will say, by Your might, You know best, I intended Your remembrance and Your Face, so He will say My servant spoke truly, take him to Paradise. > **""What did you intend by My worship? The sincere intended My Face, so take him to Paradise.""**"
"18398","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that on the Day of Resurrection the disbeliever will be known by his deeds yet deny and dispute. Told his neighbours testify, he will call them liars, likewise his family and kin, and demand oaths, so they will swear, then Allāh will silence them and their tongues will testify, then admit them to the Fire. > **""The disbeliever will deny until his own tongue testifies, then he enters the Fire.""**"
"18399","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say the first bone of man to speak on the Day mouths are sealed will be his thigh of the left leg. > **""The first bone to speak when mouths are sealed will be the thigh of the left leg.""**"
"18400","Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥaydah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, who said why should he not hold them back by their waist-wrappers from the Fire. His Lord will summon and ask him whether he conveyed to His servants, and he will say, my Lord, I conveyed, so let the present convey to the absent. Then they will be summoned with mouths bridled, and the first of them to disclose will be the thigh and the palm. Asked whether this is their religion, he said this is your religion, and wherever you do good it suffices you. > **""You will be summoned with mouths bridled, and the first to disclose will be the thigh and the palm.""**"
"18401","It was recalled that ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say Allāh will gather servants on the Day of Resurrection naked, uncircumcised, with nothing, then call them with a voice heard by far and near: I am the Requiter, I am the Sovereign, none of Hell's people should enter Fire while owed a right by one of Paradise until requited, nor any of Paradise enter Paradise while one of Hell is owed a right by him until requited, even a slap. Asked how with nothing, he said by good and evil deeds. > **""I am the Requiter, I am the Sovereign; even a slap shall be requited by good and evil deeds.""**"
"18402","ʿĀʾishah and some Companions of the Prophet ﷺ reported from the Prophet ﷺ that a man sat before him and said, O Messenger of Allāh, my slaves lie to me, betray me, and disobey me, while I beat and revile them; how am I concerning them? The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said it is weighed against their betrayal, disobedience, and lies and your punishment of them: if your punishment falls short it is merit for you, if equal it is even with nothing for or against you, and if exceeding then the excess will be requited from you for them. The man began weeping and crying out, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said what is with him, does he not recite Allāh's Book that just balances will be set for the Day of Resurrection with no soul wronged, even a mustard seed's weight brought forth. > **""If your punishment exceeds their faults, the excess will be requited from you.""**"
"18403","Abū Dharr, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ sat while two goats fed, and one butted the other causing miscarriage, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ laughed. Asked what made him laugh, he said he marvelled, for by Him in Whose Hand is his soul, it will surely be requited on the Day of Resurrection. > **""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, it will surely be requited on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"18404","In a variant the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ saw two goats butting and said, O Abā Dharr, do you know why they butted? He said no, and he said but Allāh knows and will judge between them. > **""Allāh knows why they butted and will judge between them.""**"
"18405","ʿUthmān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the hornless shall surely requite from the horned on the Day of Resurrection. > **""The hornless shall surely requite from the horned on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"18406","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said creation will requite one another until the hornless from the horned, even ant from ant. > **""Creation will requite one another, even ant from ant.""**"
"18407","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the truthful confirmed Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ told him the first dispute judged on the Day of Resurrection will be two goats, horned and hornless. > **""The first dispute judged will be two goats, horned and hornless.""**"
"18408","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said from Allāh's justice on the Day of Resurrection even the hornless will requite from the horned. > **""From Allāh's justice even the hornless will requite from the horned.""**"
"18409","Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet ﷺ was in her house with a tooth-stick in hand, calling a servant girl until anger showed on his face. Umm Salamah went to the rooms and found the girl playing with a young goat, reproaching her for playing while the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ called. The girl swore by Him who sent him with truth she had not heard, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said were it not for fear of requital he would have pained her with it. > **""Were it not for fear of requital I would have pained you with this tooth-stick.""**"
"18410","In a variant he said, were it not for requital I would have struck you with this tooth-stick. > **""Were it not for requital I would have struck you with this tooth-stick.""**"
"18411","In a variant, were it not for fear of requital I would have pained you with this whip, and in al-Ṭabarānī's wording he called a servant girl without doubt and said, were it not for fear of requital on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Were it not for fear of requital on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"18412","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said no man beats his slave but he shall be requited on the Day of Resurrection. > **""No man beats his slave but he shall be requited on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"18413","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever strikes a lash unjustly shall be requited on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever strikes a lash unjustly shall be requited on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"18414","Thawbān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the Compeller, blessed and exalted, will advance on the Day of Resurrection and place His foot upon the bridge, saying by His might and majesty no wrong of a wrongdoer shall pass Him, so He will redress creation from one another until even the hornless sheep is redressed from the maimed horned sheep for a butt. > **""By My might, no wrong of a wrongdoer shall pass Me; even the hornless sheep shall be redressed.""**"
"18415","Salmān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said a man will come on the Day of Resurrection with good deeds hoping salvation thereby, but claimants he wronged will keep coming and his good deeds taken and given to the oppressed until none remains, then with seekers still present and no good deeds left, the oppressed's evil deeds will be taken and placed upon his. > **""Good deeds will be taken for the oppressed until none remains, then their sins loaded upon him.""**"
"18416","Sulaymān ibn Ḥabīb al-Muḥāribī reported that while travelling for battle through Ḥimṣ he entered the mosque, met Thābit ibn Maʿbad and Makḥūl, and went with them to the aged Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī, may Allāh be pleased with him, whose mind and speech surpassed his frail appearance. He first told them their sitting was Allāh's proof conveyed, that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ conveyed his message and his Companions conveyed what they heard, so convey what you hear: three are guaranteed by Allāh, one who goes forth in Allāh's path until admitted to Paradise or returned with reward or spoils, and one who enters his house with peace. Then he said Hell has a bridge with seven arches, the disobedient at its middle, where a servant halted at the middle arch will be asked what debts lie upon him, reciting that they shall not conceal speech from Allāh. He will confess his debts, be told to settle them, plead he owns nothing, so his good deeds will be taken until exhausted, then the seekers' evil deeds taken and loaded upon him, until men arriving with mountain-like good deeds are left with none. > **""In Hell is a bridge of seven arches; debts will be settled from good deeds, then by loading sins.""**"
"18417","Abū Umāmah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the oppressor will come on the Day of Resurrection until upon the bridge of Hell between darkness and ruggedness the oppressed meets him, recognises him and his wrong, and the oppressed will keep requiting the oppressors until stripping their good deeds, and if none remain, their evil deeds will be cast back until he is brought to the lowest depth of the Fire. > **""The oppressed will strip the oppressor's good deeds until he falls to the lowest depth of the Fire.""**"
"18418","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said there will be brought on the Day of Resurrection mountain-like wrongs and rights among people, and Allāh will keep settling them until nothing remains. > **""Mountain-like wrongs will be brought and settled until nothing remains.""**"
"18419","Abū Burdah ibn Niyār, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh will detain the debtor for his creditor as severely as anything detained, and he will say, my Lord, how shall I pay when You raised me naked and barefoot, whence? So Allāh will say their dues will be paid from your good deeds, cast upon the people's good deeds, and if insufficient, their evil deeds will be taken and cast upon his. > **""The debtor will be detained and paid from his good deeds, or burdened with their sins.""**"
"18420","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported from the Prophet ﷺ from the Trustworthy Spirit that the Lord, blessed and exalted, said the servant's evil and good deeds will be brought and requited and judged, and if a single good deed remains, Paradise will be widened for him. > **""If a single good deed remains after requital, Paradise will be widened for him.""**"
"18421","Zādhān reported that he visited ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, finding richly dressed people foremost in his gathering, and complained at being kept distant. Ibn Masʿūd drew him near upon his own carpet, then said he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say parents will hold a debt over their child, and on the Day of Resurrection they will cling to him saying, I am your child, and they will wish for even more than that. > **""Parents will hold a debt over their child and cling to him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"18422","Jahm ibn Aws reported that he heard ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Maryam, as ʿAbdullāh ibn Rustum passed in procession professing love of his company, quote Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said not to envy a sinner for a blessing, for you know not what meets him after death; with Allāh awaits him a slayer that never dies. > **""Do not envy a sinner for a blessing; with Allāh awaits him a slayer that never dies.""**"
"18423","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said may Allāh have mercy on a servant who owes his brother a wrong in person or wealth and comes seeking release before the Day of Resurrection, for there are no dīnārs or dirhams there, only good deeds. Asked what if he has none, he said the other's evils will be taken and cast upon his. > **""Seek release before the Day when there is no coin, only good deeds.""**"
"18424","Abū Mūsā, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said what Allāh concealed for a servant in the world He will not reproach him thereby on the Day of Resurrection. > **""What Allāh concealed for a servant in the world He will not reproach him thereby.""**"
"18425","Umm Hāniʾ bint Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that Allāh will gather the first and last on one plain, then a caller beneath the Throne will call, O people of tawḥīd, Allāh has pardoned you. People will rise, some clinging to others over wrongs, then a caller will call, O people of tawḥīd, let some pardon others, and upon Me is the reward. > **""O people of tawḥīd, let some pardon others, and upon Me is the reward.""**"
"18426","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said when creatures meet on the Day of Resurrection and Paradise's people enter Paradise and Fire's people the Fire, a caller will call, O people of the gathering, forgo wrongs among you, and your reward is upon Me. > **""O people of the gathering, forgo wrongs among you, and your reward is upon Me.""**"
"18427","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said none shall enter Paradise but by Allāh's mercy. Asked, not even you, he said not even he, unless Allāh enfolds him in His mercy, gesturing with his hand above his head. > **""None shall enter Paradise but by Allāh's mercy, not even I unless He enfolds me.""**"
"18428","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that none of you shall be saved by his deeds. Asked, not even you O Messenger of Allāh, he said not even he, unless Allāh enfolds him in mercy. So be sound, draw near, go forth morning and evening and part of the night, and aim steadily and you will reach. > **""None shall be saved by his deeds; be sound, draw near, and aim steadily.""**"
"18429","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, also reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said none shall be saved by his deeds. Asked, not even you, he said not even he, unless Allāh enfolds him in mercy and bounty, and were He to take him and ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, for what these two fingers earned, gesturing with forefinger and middle, they would perish. > **""None shall be saved by his deeds, not even I unless Allāh enfolds me in mercy.""**"
"18430","Abū Mūsā, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that none of you shall be saved by his deeds. Asked, not even you, he said not even he, unless Allāh enfolds him in mercy from Him. > **""None of you shall be saved by his deeds, not even I unless Allāh enfolds me.""**"
"18431","Sharīk ibn Ṭāriq, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said none of you shall enter Paradise by deeds. Asked, not even you, he said not even he, unless Allāh enfolds him in mercy and bounty from Him. > **""None shall enter Paradise by deeds, not even I unless Allāh enfolds me in mercy.""**"
"18432","Usāmah ibn Sharīk, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say none enters Paradise by his deeds. Asked, not even you, he said not even he, unless Allāh enfolds him in mercy from Him, placing his hand upon his head. > **""None enters Paradise by deeds, not even I unless Allāh enfolds me in mercy.""**"
"18433","Asad ibn Kurz, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said, O Asad ibn Kurz, you shall not enter Paradise by deeds but by Allāh's mercy. Asked, not even you, he said not even he, unless Allāh meets him with mercy from Him. > **""You shall not enter Paradise by deeds but by Allāh's mercy, nor I unless He enfolds me.""**"
"18434","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that three registers will be produced for man on the Day of Resurrection: righteous deeds, sins, and Allāh's blessings upon him. Allāh will tell the least blessing in its register to take its price from his righteous deeds, and it will consume them all, then withdraw saying, by Your might, I am unsatisfied, with sins and blessings remaining and all righteous deeds gone. When Allāh wills mercy upon a servant He will say, O servant, I have multiplied your good deeds, overlooked your sins, and gifted you My blessings. > **""The least blessing will consume all righteous deeds, then He gifts His blessings to whom He mercies.""**"
"18435","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that an Abyssinian man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allāh, you are favoured over us by colour and prophethood; if I believe and act as you, shall I be with you in Paradise? The Prophet ﷺ said yes. Then he said whoever says Lā ilāha illā Allāh has a covenant with Allāh, and whoever says glory to Allāh has a hundred good deeds written. They asked how can we perish after this, and the Prophet ﷺ said by Him in Whose Hand is his soul a man will come on the Day of Resurrection with deeds that would weigh a mountain, yet a blessing's due will consume them, then sins remain. > **""Whoever says Lā ilāha illā Allāh has a covenant, yet a blessing's due may consume mountain-like deeds.""**"
"18436","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that Allāh will raise a servant without sin and Allāh will say whether he prefers requital by his deeds or by the blessing with him. He will say, my Lord, You know I never disobeyed You, so a blessing will be taken and consume every good deed, until he pleads, my Lord, by Your blessing and mercy, and He will say, by My blessing and mercy. > **""Shall I requite you by your deeds or by My blessing? By My blessing and mercy.""**"
"18437","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said in the seven heavens there is no foot's place, span, or palm but an angel standing, bowing, or prostrating therein, and on the Day of Resurrection they will all say, glory to You, we worshipped You not as due, though we associated nothing with You. > **""Glory to You, we worshipped You not as due, though we associated nothing with You.""**"
"18438","ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said were a man to fall upon his face from birth until aged death in Allāh's pleasure, he would deem it little on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Were a man prostrate from birth to aged death, he would deem it little that Day.""**"
"18439","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said shall he tell them the first words of Allāh to the believers on the Day of Resurrection and their first reply. They said yes, and he said Allāh will say to the believers, did you love meeting Me, and they will say yes our Lord. He will ask why, and they will say hoping His mercy and pardon, so He will say My mercy is now due to you. > **""Did you love meeting Me? My mercy is now due to you.""**"
"18440","ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that she asked, O Messenger of Allāh, will a lover remember his beloved on the Day of Resurrection, and he said, O ʿĀʾishah, not at three: at the Balance until heavy or light, at the scattering of books to right or left, and when a neck emerges from the Fire enfolding people and proclaiming it is entrusted with three, entrusted with whoever claimed another god with Allāh, whoever disbelieved in the Day of Reckoning, and every obstinate tyrant, then casting them into Hell's depths. For Hell has a bridge thinner than a hair and sharper than a sword, with hooks and thorns seizing whom Allāh wills, while people cross like a glance, lightning, wind, and fine horses and mounts, and the angels say, Lord, grant safety, so some emerge sound, some scratched yet safe, and some rolled into Fire upon their faces. > **""None remembers his beloved at the Balance, at the scattering of books, and when the neck emerges from the Fire.""**"
"18441","Abū Bakrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that people will be carried upon the Bridge on the Day of Resurrection, its sides tossing them as moths in fire, and Allāh by His mercy will save whom He wills. Then angels, prophets, and martyrs will be permitted to intercede, interceding and bringing out, interceding and bringing out, even whoever has an atom's weight of faith in his heart. > **""Its sides will toss them as moths, then intercessors will bring out even an atom of faith.""**"
"18442","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that his nation's cry when mounted upon the Bridge will be, O there is no god but You. > **""The cry of my nation upon the Bridge will be: O there is no god but You.""**"
"18443","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh will call people on the Day of Resurrection by their names, veiling His servants, but at the Bridge He will grant every believing man and woman a light, and every hypocrite a light. When levelled upon the Bridge, Allāh will strip the hypocrites' light, so they will say, wait for us to borrow from your light, while believers say, our Lord, perfect our light, and none shall remember another then. > **""Each believer is given a light, then the hypocrites' light is stripped at the Bridge.""**"
"18444","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said the Bridge will be laid level over Hell like a sharp sword's edge, slippery and sliding, with hooks of fire snatching thereby. Some will be seized falling therein, some struck down, some passing like lightning to safety, then like wind, then a horse's run, a man's run, a man's trot, and a man's walk. The last will be a man whom Fire has earned and met with evil, until Allāh admits him to Paradise by mercy. Told to wish and ask, he will say, my Lord, do You mock me while Lord of might, and be told again to wish and ask, until his wishes end and he is told, yours is what you asked and its like with it. > **""Some will pass like lightning, like wind, like a horse's run, until the last is told: yours is what you asked and its like.""**"
"18445","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, added, and ten times its like with it. > **""And ten times its like with him.""**"
"18446","Yaʿlā ibn Munabbih, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the Fire will say to the believers on the Day of Resurrection, pass O believer, for your light has quenched my flame. > **""Pass O believer, for your light has quenched my flame.""**"
"18447","Abū Sumaynah reported that they disputed here over the passing, some saying no believer enters it, others saying all enter then Allāh saves the God-fearing. So he met Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allāh be pleased with them, who pointed to his ears and swore deafness if he had not heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say the passing is the entering: none righteous or wicked but shall enter it, and it will be coolness and safety upon believers as it was upon Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, until the Fire clamours from their coolness, then Allāh saves the God-fearing and leaves the wrongdoers therein. > **""The passing is the entering; it will be coolness and safety upon the believers.""**"
"18448","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Hell's heat upon his nation is but like a bathhouse's heat. > **""Hell's heat upon my nation is but like a bathhouse's heat.""**"
"18449","Abū Umāmah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said there shall surely come upon Hell a day when it is as withered reddened crops, its gates clattering. > **""There shall come upon Hell a day when it is as withered reddened crops.""**"
"18450","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he visited the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, who said he had been given al-Kawthar. Asked what it is, he said a river in Paradise its width and length between east and west, none drinks from it then thirsts, none performs ablution from it then is dishevelled, and none drinks who violated his covenant or killed his Household. > **""I have been given al-Kawthar, a river between east and west denied to covenant-breakers.""**"
"18451","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said his Basin stretches from one place to another, with vessels as numerous as stars, more fragrant than musk, sweeter than honey, cooler than snow, and whiter than milk; whoever drinks a draught never thirsts again. > **""My Basin has vessels as numerous as stars; whoever drinks never thirsts again.""**"
"18452","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say, O company of Anṣār, your meeting place is my Basin. > **""O company of Anṣār, your meeting place is my Basin.""**"
"18453","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he has a river between Ṣanʿāʾ and Aylah, with vessels as numerous as stars, cooler than snow, sweeter than honey, whiter than milk; whoever drinks a draught never thirsts thereafter, and whoever does not taste it is never quenched. > **""I have a river between Ṣanʿāʾ and Aylah; whoever drinks never thirsts thereafter.""**"
"18454","ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah reported that ʿAbdullāh ibn Ziyād doubted the Basin, so he sent to Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allāh be pleased with him, asking about it, and he narrated an adorned report that pleased him. Asked whether he heard it from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, he said no, but his brother narrated it to him. > **""Zayd ibn Arqam narrated an adorned report on the Basin from his brother.""**"
"18455","Yuḥannis reported that Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, may Allāh be pleased with him, upon reaching Madīnah married Khawlah bint Qays ibn Fahd al-Anṣāriyyah of Banū al-Najjār, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would visit Ḥamzah in her house, while she narrated reports from him ﷺ. She said the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came to them and she asked whether he spoke of a Basin on the Day of Resurrection spanning such and such, and he said yes, with her people most beloved to him to drink from it. She then offered a pot with bread or porridge, and when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ put his hand in to eat his fingers were burnt, so he said ḥass, then said man when struck by cold says ḥass, and when struck by heat says ḥass. > **""Yes, I have a Basin, and most beloved to drink from it are your people.""**"
"18456","Khawlah bint Ḥakīm, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that she asked, O Messenger of Allāh, do you have a Basin, and he said yes, with her people most beloved to him to come to it. > **""Yes, and most beloved to come to it are your people.""**"
"18457","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the two sons of Mulaykah came to the Prophet ﷺ and said their mother honoured husbands, pitied children, and fed guests, yet buried a daughter alive in Jāhiliyyah. He said their mother is in the Fire, so they turned with evil upon their faces, then were called back with joy hoping something new had come, and he said my mother is with your mothers. A hypocrite remarked what avails this for his own mother while they tread his heels, so an Anṣārī, most questioning of the Prophet ﷺ, asked whether any promise concerned her. He thought it from something heard, and the Prophet ﷺ said he had not asked his Lord nor been given hope thereof, but he shall stand the Praiseworthy Station on the Day of Resurrection. Asked what it is, he said when you are brought barefoot, naked, uncircumcised, Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, will be clothed first as the Friend, given two white wraps and seated facing the Throne, then he ﷺ will be clothed and stand at his right in a station none other holds, envied by first and last. A river will be opened from al-Kawthar to the Basin, whose bed is musk and pebbles garlic-like, questioned by the hypocrite yet affirmed with growth of golden rods and fruits of jewels, its water whiter than milk and sweeter than honey, whoever drinks never thirsting thereafter, and whoever is denied never quenched. > **""I shall stand the Praiseworthy Station, and whoever drinks from my Basin never thirsts thereafter.""**"
"18458","Abū Umāmah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh promised to admit seventy thousand of his nation to Paradise without reckoning. Yazīd ibn al-Akhnas said they are but as a reddish fly among flies, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said his Lord promised seventy thousand, with every thousand seventy thousand, plus three handfuls. Asked the Basin's extent, he said between ʿAdan and ʿUmān and wider, gesturing with his hand, with two spouts of gold and silver. Asked its drink, he said whiter than milk, sweeter than honey, more fragrant than musk; whoever drinks never thirsts thereafter nor blackens in face ever. > **""My Lord promised seventy thousand, with every thousand seventy thousand, plus three handfuls.""**"
"18459","Usāmah ibn Zayd, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Prophet ﷺ visited Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib one day and missed him, asking his wife of Banū al-Najjār, who said he had gone toward him missing him in their lanes, inviting him inside. She offered ḥays and he ate, and she congratulated him, for Abū ʿUmārah told her he was given a river in Paradise called al-Kawthar. He said yes, its expanse of ruby, coral, peridot, and pearl. She asked for a description of his Basin, and he said between Aylah and Ṣanʿāʾ, with pitchers as numerous as stars, and most beloved to come to it are your people, O daughter of Ḥamd, meaning the Anṣār. > **""My Basin is between Aylah and Ṣanʿāʾ, and most beloved to it are your people.""**"
"18460","Ḥudhayfah ibn Asīd al-Ghifārī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said, O people, I am your forerunner and you shall come to the Basin, its width between Ṣanʿāʾ and Buṣrā, with cups of gold and silver as numerous as stars. He shall ask them at its coming about the two weighty things, so see how you succeed him therein: the greater cause, Allāh's Book, a rope with one end in Allāh's Hand and one in theirs, so hold fast, neither straying nor altering, and his progeny, his Household, for the All-Knowing informed him they shall never end until reaching him at the Basin. > **""I am your forerunner at the Basin; hold to Allāh's Book and my Household until they reach me.""**"
"18461","Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said prophets will boast which has most companions from his nation, and he hopes to be the greatest in arrivals that Day. Each prophet will stand at a full basin with a staff, calling those he knows of his nation, and every nation has a mark by which its prophet knows them. > **""Prophets will boast of multitudes, and I hope to be greatest in arrivals.""**"
"18462","Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he is your forerunner at the Basin; whoever comes and drinks never thirsts again, yet peoples he recognises shall come and then be barred from him. > **""I am your forerunner at the Basin; whoever drinks never thirsts again.""**"
"18463","Sahl, may Allāh be pleased with him, also reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said every one has a forerunner, and he is your forerunner at the Basin; whoever comes to the Basin and drinks never thirsts, and whoever never thirsts enters Paradise. > **""Whoever comes to my Basin and drinks never thirsts, and enters Paradise.""**"
"18464","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say what is with men who claim the kinship of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ avails not his people; indeed by Allāh his kinship is joined in world and Hereafter, and he is your forerunner at the Basin. When they come, one will say I am so-and-so son of so-and-so, and he will say lineages are known, but you innovated after me and turned back on your heels. > **""My kinship is joined in world and Hereafter, but you innovated after me and turned back.""**"
"18465","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say he is your forerunner at the Basin; whoever comes prospers, but peoples will be brought and seized to the left, so he will say, my Lord, and be told they kept turning apostate on their heels after him. > **""I am your forerunner; whoever comes prospers, but apostates are seized to the left.""**"
"18466","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he shall stand at the Basin watching who comes, and men of his own will be seized, so he will say, my Lord, my nation, my nation, and be told, what do you know of what they did after you; they kept turning on their heels after you. Jābir added that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the Basin is a month's journey, its corners equal, length as width, cups as stars of the sky, more fragrant than musk, whiter than milk; whoever drinks never thirsts thereafter. > **""My nation, my nation; they kept turning on their heels after you.""**"
"18467","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with them, also heard the Prophet ﷺ say he is your forerunner before you; if you do not see him, he is at the Basin spanning Aylah to Makkah, yet men and women shall come with skins and vessels and taste nothing. > **""I am your forerunner at the Basin between Aylah and Makkah.""**"
"18468","Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said peoples who were with him shall come, and when they raise their heads they will be snatched from him, so he will say, my companions, my companions, and be told he knows not what they innovated after him. > **""My companions, my companions; you know not what they innovated after you.""**"
"18469","Abū Bakrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said men who accompanied and saw him shall surely come to the Basin, and when raised to him and seen they will be snatched from him, so he will say, my Lord, my companions, my companions, and be told he knows not what they innovated after him. > **""Men who accompanied me will be snatched from me; you know not what they innovated after you.""**"
"18470","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he shall surely find himself disputing for one of them at the Basin, saying they are of his companions, and be told he knows not what they innovated after him. Abū al-Dardāʾ asked him to pray Allāh not make him among them, and he said you are not among them. > **""You know not what they innovated after you; you are not among them.""**"
"18471","Abū Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that men of his companions shall surely be raised to him, and when he sees them they will be snatched from him, so he will say, my companions, and be told he knows not what they innovated after him. > **""My companions; you know not what they innovated after you.""**"
"18472","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he is your forerunner at the Basin and shall multiply the nations through you, so do not return after him as disbelievers striking one another's necks. Asked its width, he said between Aylah and Makkah, with cups more numerous than stars; no believer takes one and lays it down but another takes it. > **""I am your forerunner and shall multiply nations through you, so do not strike one another's necks.""**"
"18473","Al-Farazdaq reported that Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, told him, O Farazdaq, I see your feet small, so if you can secure for them a place by the Basin then do, for he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say his Basin is between ʿUmān and Aylah, its water whiter than milk and sweeter than honey, its vessels as numerous as stars; whoever drinks never thirsts ever. > **""My Basin is between ʿUmān and Aylah; whoever drinks never thirsts ever.""**"
"18474","In a variant he said he has a Basin to which his nation shall come, spanning as between Ṣanʿāʾ and Yathrib. > **""I have a Basin spanning as between Ṣanʿāʾ and Yathrib.""**"
"18475","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he has a Basin and is your forerunner thereon. > **""I have a Basin and am your forerunner thereon.""**"
"18476","Al-ʿIrbāḍ ibn Sāriyah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said this nation shall surely crowd at the Basin as camels crowd arriving after five days' thirst. > **""This nation shall crowd at the Basin as thirsty camels crowd.""**"
"18477","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said his Basin spans as between ʿAdan and ʿUmān, cooler than snow, sweeter than honey, more fragrant than musk, with cups like stars; whoever drinks a draught never thirsts thereafter. The first to come shall be the poor emigrants: dishevelled heads, pale faces, soiled garments, gates unopened to them, marrying not the pampered, giving all dues upon them while taking not all dues for them. > **""The first to come to my Basin shall be the poor emigrants.""**"
"18478","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that his Basin spans as between ʿAdan and ʿUmān, with cups as numerous as stars; whoever drinks never thirsts thereafter. Among his nation coming to him shall be the dishevelled, soiled in garments, marrying not the pampered, attending not rulers' gates, giving all dues upon them while granted not all dues for them. > **""Whoever drinks never thirsts; the dishevelled who give all dues shall come to it.""**"
"18479","Thawbān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he shall drive people from his Basin for his Household, striking them with this staff of his until it overflows. > **""I shall drive people from my Basin for my Household with this staff.""**"
"18480","Buraydah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that mentioning the Basin he said therein are seen pitchers as numerous as the stars of the sky. > **""In it are pitchers as numerous as the stars of the sky.""**"
"18481","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he was given al-Kawthar, and striking with his hand found it strong fragrant musk, its pebbles pearl, and its banks, he thought domes, flowing upon the earth unsplit. > **""I was given al-Kawthar of fragrant musk, pearl pebbles, and domed banks.""**"
"18482","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said his Basin is a month's journey, equal-sided, cups as numerous as stars, water whiter than snow, sweeter than honey, more fragrant than musk; whoever drinks a draught never thirsts thereafter. > **""My Basin is a month's journey; whoever drinks never thirsts thereafter.""**"
"18483","Abū Barzah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say his Basin spans Aylah to Ṣanʿāʾ, width as length, into which two spouts pour from Paradise, one of silver and one of gold. It is whiter than milk, sweeter than honey, cooler than snow, with pitchers as numerous as stars; whoever drinks never thirsts until entering Paradise. > **""Two spouts pour into it from Paradise; whoever drinks never thirsts until Paradise.""**"
"18484","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said his Basin spans Aylah to Ṣanʿāʾ, with two spouts, one of gold and one of silver, vessels as numerous as stars, whiter than milk, sweeter than honey, more fragrant than musk; whoever drinks never thirsts ever. > **""Whoever drinks from it never thirsts ever.""**"
"18485","Abū Hurayrah and Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, is the keeper of his Basin on the Day of Resurrection, with cups as numerous as stars, and its expanse between al-Jābiyah and Ṣanʿāʾ. > **""ʿAlī is the keeper of my Basin, with cups as numerous as stars.""**"
"18486","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that in the year of Tabūk the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ rose at night to pray while men of his Companions guarded him, and turning to them said he was granted that night five never granted before: sent to all mankind while those before were sent to their peoples, aided by dread across a month's journey, spoils made lawful to eat while those before magnified and burnt them, the earth made mosques and purification wherever prayer overtakes, and a fifth concerning intercession. > **""I was sent to all mankind, aided by dread, given spoils and the earth as mosque.""**"
"18487","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Companions once missed the Prophet ﷺ, for they left him mid-camp when halting, and feared Allāh had chosen others. Seeing his form they magnified Allāh and said they feared replacement, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said no, rather you are his companions in world and Hereafter. Allāh awakened him saying no prophet or messenger asked a request but was given, and he was offered intercession for his nation. > **""No, you are my companions in world and Hereafter; I chose intercession for my nation.""**"
"18488","Muʿādh ibn Jabal and Abū Mūsā, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ halted, the emigrants stayed nearest him. One night he and Muʿādh awoke missing him, sought him hearing a hum like millstones, and he approached asking what ailed them. They said they feared harm had befallen him, so he said a comer in sleep gave him choice between half his nation entering Paradise or intercession, and he chose intercession. > **""I was given choice between half my nation in Paradise or intercession, and chose intercession.""**"
"18489","In Aḥmad's variant they said, pray Allāh to place us in your intercession, and he said you and whoever dies associating nothing with Allāh are in his intercession. > **""You and whoever dies associating nothing with Allāh are in my intercession.""**"
"18490","Abū Mūsā, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that they campaigned with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ on a journey, and at night he sought the Prophet's resting place missing him, finding another Companion seeking likewise. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came toward them and they said he was in hostile land unguarded, suggesting he take a companion when answering need. He told them of glad tidings of intercession granted that night. > **""I bring glad tidings of intercession granted this night.""**"
"18491","In a variant with him, they marched near dawn, halted and gathered about him as customary, he hobbled his camel, laid his cheek upon its hobble, slept, and dispersed. Raising his head one found him gone and feared, then heard like a mill's hum from the valley, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came joyful with the choice of intercession. > **""He came joyful with the choice of intercession for his nation.""**"
"18492","Muṣʿab al-Aslamī reported that a youth came to the Prophet ﷺ asking to be placed among those he intercedes for on the Day of Resurrection. He asked who commanded, taught, or guided him to this, and he said none but himself. He said then you are among those he intercedes for that Day, and the youth went joyful to his family. When he turned, he said return the youth, and they returned him dejected fearing something new, and he said aid him against himself with abundant prostration. > **""You are among those I intercede for, so aid yourself with abundant prostration.""**"
"18493","ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that travelling with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, his eyes stayed sleepless at night. Rising, he saw no beast but cheek to earth, sensing every thing fallen at saddle-backs, and threading through, found the Prophet's bed empty and cold, crying to Allāh we belong to Him and return. Then the Prophet ﷺ came reporting the choice between half his nation in Paradise or intercession. > **""I was given choice between half my nation in Paradise or intercession.""**"
"18494","In another variant from ʿAwf, halting with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, he awoke seeing nothing taller than a saddle-back, every man and camel clinging to earth. Reaching the Prophet's bed missing and cold, he threaded people crying to Allāh, then heard the like except he said he was given choice to admit half his nation to Paradise. > **""He was given choice to admit half his nation or intercede, and chose intercession.""**"
"18495","In a variant Abū Mūsā was placed where Abū ʿUbaydah stood in the chain of the same report. > **""The same report with Abū Mūsā in place of Abū ʿUbaydah.""**"
"18496","Abū Kaʿb the silk-merchant reported that he asked al-Naḍr ibn Anas to narrate a report by which Allāh benefits him concerning intercession and the Basin. > **""Narrate to me a report by which Allāh benefits me concerning intercession.""**"
"18497","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿUqayl reported that he came in a delegation to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, halting at the door hating the man they would enter upon, and leaving loving him most among people. One asked why he did not ask his Lord for a kingdom like Sulaymān's, peace be upon him, so he laughed and said perhaps your companion holds better with Allāh than Sulaymān's kingdom. Every prophet was given a supplication, some taking worldly gain, some invoking against disobedient peoples unto ruin, while he reserved his for intercession. > **""Every prophet was given a supplication, and I reserved mine for intercession.""**"
"18498","Abū Saʿīd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said every prophet was given a gift and hastened it, but he reserved his gift as intercession for his nation. > **""Every prophet hastened his gift, but I reserved mine as intercession for my nation.""**"
"18499","Abū Dharr, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he was given five never granted before: the earth made purification and mosque, spoils made lawful never lawful to a prophet before, aided by dread a month against his enemy, sent to every red and black, and given intercession reaching whoever of his nation associates nothing with Allāh. > **""I was given five never granted before, and intercession reaching unassociating monotheists.""**"
"18500","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he was given five never granted to a prophet before: sent to red and black while those before were sent to their peoples, the earth made mosque and purification, aided by dread a month ahead, spoils made lawful to him never lawful before, and given intercession which he deferred for his nation, reaching whoever associates nothing with Allāh. > **""I was given five never granted before, and deferred intercession for whoever associates nothing with Allāh.""**"
"18501","Umm Salamah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he saw what his nation would do after him, so he chose for them intercession on the Day of Resurrection. > **""I saw what my nation would do after me, so I chose intercession for them.""**"
"18502","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said a man will keep begging people until he comes on the Day of Resurrection with no morsel of flesh on his face. He also said the sun will draw near until sweat reaches half the ear, and while thus they will seek Ādam, peace be upon him, who will say he is not its man, then Mūsā likewise, then Muḥammad ﷺ, who will intercede and Allāh will judge creation. He will walk to grasp Paradise's ring, and that Day Allāh will raise him to the Praiseworthy Station praised by all the gathering. > **""Then Muḥammad ﷺ will intercede, grasp Paradise's ring, and be raised to the Praiseworthy Station.""**"
"18503","Salmān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the sun on the Day of Resurrection will be given ten years' heat then drawn near skulls. They will come to the Prophet ﷺ saying, O Prophet of Allāh, you by whom Allāh opened and forgave past and future sins, you see our plight, so intercede for us. He will say, I am your man, going forth herding people to Paradise's gate, grasping a golden ring and knocking. Asked who, he will say Muḥammad, and it will be opened until he stands before Allāh, prostrates, and is told to raise his head. > **""I am your man; I shall knock at Paradise's gate until opened for me.""**"
"18504","Abū Naḍrah reported that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, address from Baṣrah's pulpit that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said every prophet had a supplication hastened in the world, but he reserved his as intercession for his nation on the Day of Resurrection. He is the master of Ādam's children without boast, first over whom the earth splits without boast, in his hand the banner of praise with Ādam and below beneath it without boast. The Day will lengthen and harden until people say go to Ādam, father of mankind, to intercede for judgment, and they will go to Ādam, then through the prophets, until reaching him ﷺ. > **""I reserved my supplication as intercession, and in my hand is the banner of praise.""**"
"18505","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he shall knock at Paradise's gate and a golden gate with silver ring will open, the greatest light meeting him, and he will fall prostrate, granted praise upon Allāh never granted before. Told to raise his head, ask and be given, speak and be heard, intercede and be granted, he will say, my nation, and be told his are those with a barley grain of faith. Then a second prostration for those with a mustard seed of faith, then a third likewise for even less. > **""I shall knock at Paradise's gate and prostrate, saying: My nation, my nation.""**"
"18506","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet of Allāh ﷺ told him he shall stand awaiting his nation crossing the Bridge when ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, comes saying the prophets have come to him, O Muḥammad, gathering to him to pray Allāh separate all nations as He wills for the anguish they suffer, with creation bridled in sweat, upon the believer as a cold, upon the disbeliever engulfing as death. ʿĪsā will say wait until he returns, then the Prophet of Allāh ﷺ will go stand beneath the Throne meeting what no chosen angel met. > **""I shall stand awaiting my nation crossing the Bridge while creation is bridled in sweat.""**"
"18507","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ one morning prayed dawn, sat until forenoon laughing, then remained silent through Ẓuhr, ʿAṣr, Maghrib, and ʿIshāʾ, then rose home. People asked Abū Bakr to question this unprecedented conduct, so he asked and he said yes, there was shown to him what shall be of world and Hereafter: first and last gathered on one plain, and the terrors of judgment were detailed. > **""There was shown to me what shall be; first and last gathered on one plain.""**"
"18508","Abū Ayyūb, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ told them his Lord gave him choice between seventy thousand entering Paradise pardoned without reckoning or the stored intercession with Him for his nation. Asked whether the Lord would store that, he entered then emerged magnifying Allāh, saying his Lord added with every thousand seventy thousand, plus the stored intercession. Abū Ruwaym asked Abū Ayyūb what he thought that store was, and people pressed him for answer. > **""My Lord gave me seventy thousand pardoned, with every thousand seventy thousand, plus the stored intercession.""**"
"18509","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Household of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had a servant named Burrah, whom a man met saying, O Burrah, cover your hair, for Muḥammad shall avail you nothing with Allāh. She told the Prophet ﷺ, who emerged trailing his cloak with reddened cheeks, by which the Anṣār knew his anger, so they took arms and pledged obedience even against parents and children. He ascended the pulpit, praised Allāh, and affirmed his kinship and intercession avail, warning against violating his Household. > **""My kinship and intercession avail; whoever violates my Household violates me.""**"
"18510","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that when Allāh gathers first and last, judges, and finishes judgment, the believers will say judgment is passed and they seek one to intercede to their Lord. They will go to Ādam, peace be upon him, as father created by Allāh's Hand and spoken to, but he will send them to Nūḥ, who will guide to Ibrāhīm, who to Mūsā, who to ʿĪsā, peace be upon them, who will say he guides them to the unlettered Prophet. They will come to him ﷺ, and Allāh will permit him to intercede. > **""They will go from Ādam to Nūḥ to Ibrāhīm to Mūsā to ʿĪsā, then to the unlettered Prophet for intercession.""**"
"18511","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said countless of this qiblah none but Allāh numbers shall enter Fire for disobeying Allāh, daring disobedience, and opposing obedience. Then he ﷺ will be permitted intercession, praising Allāh prostrate as standing, and be told to raise his head, ask and be given, intercede and be granted. > **""Then I shall be permitted intercession; raise your head, ask and be given, intercede and be granted.""**"
"18512","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said by Him in Whose Hand is his soul he is surely master of mankind on the Day of Resurrection without boast or show, all mankind beneath his banner awaiting relief, with him the banner of praise. He will walk with people to Paradise's gate, seek opening, announce Muḥammad, be welcomed, and upon seeing his Lord fall thankful prostrate. Told to raise his head, speak and be obeyed, intercede and be granted, those enveloped by mercy and his intercession shall emerge. > **""I am master of mankind without boast, with the banner of praise, and intercession shall be granted.""**"
"18513","Kaʿb ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said people will be raised on the Day of Resurrection with him and his nation upon a hill. His Lord will clothe him in a green robe, then permit him to praise Him as He deserves, and that is the Praiseworthy Station. > **""He and his nation shall be upon a hill, and that is the Praiseworthy Station.""**"
"18514","Ḥudhayfah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said he is master of mankind on the Day of Resurrection, his Lord shall call him and he will say, at Your service and blessed aid, good is in Your Hands, blessed and exalted are You, at Your service among those You shield, the guided is whom You guide, Your servant before You, no refuge nor escape from You but to You, blessed and exalted are You. > **""I am master of mankind; at Your service, no refuge from You but to You.""**"
"18515","Ḥudhayfah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that Allāh will gather people on one plain with no soul speaking, and the first, he thought, to speak will be Muḥammad ﷺ, saying, at Your service and blessed aid, good is in Your Hands and evil is not unto You, the guided is whom You guide, Your servant before You, from You and to You, no refuge nor escape from You but to You, blessed and exalted, glory to You Lord of the House. And this is His word that your Lord may raise you to the Praiseworthy Station. > **""The first to speak shall be Muḥammad ﷺ, and that is the Praiseworthy Station.""**"
"18516","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he shall intercede for his nation until his Lord, blessed and exalted, calls to him, are you pleased O Muḥammad, and he will say, indeed my Lord, I am pleased. > **""I shall intercede until my Lord says: Are you pleased, O Muḥammad?""**"
"18517","ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that while they sat in the Prophet's courtyard he emerged radiant and cheerful, and they rejoiced at his brightness. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Jibrīl, peace be upon him, just brought glad tidings that Allāh granted him intercession. Asked whether for Banū Hāshim or Quraysh particularly, he said no, then whether for his nation, he said it is for his nation, for burdened sinners. > **""Allāh granted me intercession for my nation, for burdened sinners.""**"
"18518","Abū Umāmah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he is the best of men for the worst of his nation. Asked how for their best, he said as for their worst Allāh shall admit them to Paradise by his intercession, while their best Allāh shall admit by their deeds. > **""I am the best of men for the worst of my nation; they enter Paradise by my intercession.""**"
"18519","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said one day his intercession is for grave sinners of his nation. Ibn ʿAbbās added the foremost in good enter Paradise without reckoning, the moderate by Allāh's mercy, and self-wrongers and the people of the Heights by Muḥammad's intercession ﷺ. > **""My intercession is for grave sinners; self-wrongers enter Paradise thereby.""**"
"18520","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he was given choice between intercession or half his nation in Paradise, and chose intercession as wider and more sufficient. Do you deem it for the God-fearing? No, but for stained sinners. Ziyād remarked it is yet for them, but thus narrated their narrator. > **""I chose intercession as wider; it is for stained sinners, not only the God-fearing.""**"
"18521","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said his intercession is for grave sinners of his nation. > **""My intercession is for grave sinners of my nation.""**"
"18522","In a variant in both collections, intercession was appointed only for grave sinners of his nation. > **""Intercession was appointed only for grave sinners of my nation.""**"
"18523","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that they withheld seeking pardon for grave sinners until they heard their Prophet ﷺ say he reserved his intercession for grave sinners of his nation on the Day of Resurrection. So they held back from much within themselves and hoped for them. > **""He reserved his intercession for grave sinners, so we hoped for them.""**"
"18524","Umm Salamah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said work and rely not, for his intercession is for the perishing of his nation. > **""Work and rely not, for my intercession is for the perishing of my nation.""**"
"18525","Buraydah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he visited Muʿāwiyah finding a man speaking, and sought leave to speak. Granted, though thought to echo the other, Buraydah said he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say he hopes to intercede on the Day of Resurrection as many as earth's trees and clods. He said, do you hope it O Muʿāwiyah, while ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, hopes it not? > **""I hope to intercede as many as earth's trees and clods.""**"
"18526","Buraydah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ ask thrice, are stones and trees many? They said yes, and he said by Him in Whose Hand is his soul, his intercession is more than stones and trees. > **""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, my intercession is more than stones and trees.""**"
"18527","Unays al-Anṣārī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say he shall surely intercede on the Day of Resurrection in all upon earth's face of stone and clod. > **""I shall intercede in all upon earth's face of stone and clod.""**"
"18528","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he shall come to Hell's gate and strike it, and it will open for him. He will enter praising Allāh with praises none before or after matched, then bring out whoever said Lā ilāha illā Allāh sincerely. Men of Quraysh will rise claiming kinship, and he will know their lineage but not their faces, leaving them in the Fire. > **""I shall strike Hell's gate and bring out whoever said Lā ilāha illā Allāh sincerely.""**"
"18529","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Paradise's people will miss men they knew in the world, come to the prophets mentioning them, and they will intercede and be granted. They will be called the freed, all freed, with the water of life poured over them. > **""They will intercede for the missed and be granted; they are called the freed.""**"
"18530","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said some Muslims once tormented in the Fire shall surely enter Paradise by Allāh's mercy and the intercession of intercessors. > **""Some Muslims once tormented in the Fire shall surely enter Paradise by mercy and intercession.""**"
"18531","Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said peoples shall emerge from the Fire and be named in Paradise the Hell-dwellers. They will pray Allāh to remove that name, and Allāh will erase it from them, for emerging from the Fire they sprout as feathers sprout. > **""They shall emerge from the Fire, be named Hell-dwellers, then Allāh will erase that name.""**"
"18532","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said some of his nation shall be tormented for sins, remaining in Fire as long as Allāh wills, then pagans will taunt them saying their belief availed nothing. So no monotheist shall remain but Allāh will bring him out. Then the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ recited that disbelievers shall wish they were Muslims. > **""No monotheist shall remain in the Fire but Allāh will bring him out.""**"
"18533","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said some of the people of Lā ilāha illā Allāh shall enter Fire for sins, and the people of al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā will say their declaration availed nothing while with them in Fire. So Allāh will anger for them, bring them out and cast them into the river of life, healing their burns as the moon heals from eclipse. They will enter Paradise, with Paradise's people naming them the Hell-dwellers. Asked whether he heard this from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, he swore he did. > **""Allāh will anger for them, cast them into the river of life, and admit them to Paradise.""**"
"18534","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh, mighty and majestic, will say to bring out of the Fire whoever had a barley grain's weight of faith in his heart, then whoever had a mustard seed's weight of faith, then by His might and majesty He will not make one who believed in Him an hour of night or day as one who never believed. > **""Bring out whoever had a barley or mustard seed of faith; the momentary believer is not as the disbeliever.""**"
"18535","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said intercession shall keep reaching people emerging from the Fire until even Iblīs, chief of devils, stretches toward it hoping to attain it. > **""Intercession shall keep reaching them until even Iblīs stretches hoping for it.""**"
"18536","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said pulpits of light will be set for the prophets to sit upon, while his pulpit remains unseated, for he shall stand before his Lord occupied with his nation, fearing to be sent to Paradise while his nation remains behind. He will say, my Lord, my nation, my nation, and Allāh will ask what he wishes done for them. He will say, hasten their reckoning, so they will be summoned and reckoned: some entering Paradise by His mercy, some entering Fire then emerging by intercession. > **""My pulpit remains unseated while I stand for my nation; my Lord, my nation.""**"
"18537","Ḥudhayfah, may Allāh be pleased with him, raised to the Prophet ﷺ that Allāh will bring out peoples, foul and scorched by Fire, through intercessors' intercession. They will enter Paradise named the Hell-dwellers. > **""Foul scorched peoples shall be brought out by intercessors into Paradise.""**"
"18538","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the first he intercedes for of his nation are his Household, then nearest then nearest of Quraysh and the Anṣār, then Yemenis who believed and followed him, then remaining Arabs, then non-Arabs, and the first interceded are the virtuous. > **""The first I intercede for are my Household, then nearest of Quraysh and Anṣār.""**"
"18539","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿAbbād ibn Jaʿfar reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say the first he intercedes for of his nation are the people of Madīnah, Makkah, and Ṭāʾif. > **""The first I intercede for are the people of Madīnah, Makkah, and Ṭāʾif.""**"
"18540","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh will grant Ādam, peace be upon him, intercession on the Day of Resurrection for all his offspring, for a hundred thousand thousand and ten thousand thousand. > **""Allāh will grant Ādam intercession for myriads of his offspring.""**"
"18541","Kharashah ibn al-Ḥurr reported that arriving in Madīnah he met ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām, may Allāh be pleased with him, who offered a report in Allāh's Book, mentioning peoples emerging from the Fire while Ādam, peace be upon him, says, my Lord, my children are burnt, and they are brought out. > **""My Lord, my children are burnt, so they are brought out from the Fire.""**"
"18542","ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the first to intercede on the Day of Resurrection are the prophets, then the martyrs, then the callers to prayer. > **""The first to intercede are the prophets, then the martyrs, then the callers.""**"
"18543","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said fasting and Qurʾān shall intercede for the servant on the Day of Resurrection. Fasting will say, my Lord, I barred him from food and desire, so grant me intercession for him. Qurʾān will say, I barred him from sleep at night, so grant me intercession for him. And they shall both intercede. > **""Fasting and Qurʾān shall intercede, saying: I barred him, so grant me intercession.""**"
"18544","Abū Umāmah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say there shall surely enter Paradise through one man not a prophet as many as the two tribes Rabīʿah and Muḍar. Asked whether Rabīʿah is of Muḍar, he said he says only what he says. > **""Through one man not a prophet shall enter as many as Rabīʿah and Muḍar.""**"
"18545","Abū Barzah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say among his nation are those who intercede for more than Rabīʿah and Muḍar, and among his nation are those magnified for the Fire until a pillar thereof. > **""Among my nation are those interceding for more than Rabīʿah and Muḍar.""**"
"18546","Abū Umāmah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said there shall enter Paradise through one man of his nation more than the number of Muḍar, a man intercedes for his household, and intercedes according to his deeds. > **""Through one man shall enter more than Muḍar, each interceding by household and deeds.""**"
"18547","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said Fire's people shall be displayed in rows on the Day of Resurrection, and believers will pass them. A man of the Fire recognising a believer from the world will say, O so-and-so, do you remember when you sought my aid for such a need, and the believer will recall, recognise him, intercede to his Lord, and be granted for him. > **""The believer will recall a worldly favour and intercede, and be granted.""**"
"18548","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said a man shall surely intercede for two or three men. > **""A man shall surely intercede for two or three.""**"
"18549","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that two men crossed a desert, one a worshipper and the other burdened with sin. The worshipper thirsted until fallen, while his companion with a little water in a vessel looked upon him prostrate and swore by Allāh that if this righteous servant dies thirsty with water at hand he shall attain no good from Allāh, yet giving his water means his own death. So relying upon Allāh he resolved, sprinkled and gave him from his surplus, and he rose to cross the desert. He shall be halted for requital for that draught. > **""Relying upon Allāh he gave his surplus water, and shall be requited for that draught.""**"
"18550","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said a man of Paradise shall overlook the Fire's people, and a man of the Fire will call, O so-and-so, do you know me? He will say, no by Allāh, who are you, woe to you? He will say, I am he past whom you walked in the world and you asked me for a drink and I gave you, so intercede for me thereby with your Lord. That man will enter to his Lord in His abodes saying, my Lord, I overlooked the Fire and a man therein called me for a draught I once gave, so intercede for him. > **""I gave you a draught in the world, so intercede for me thereby with your Lord.""**"
"18551","Shuraḥbīl ibn Shufʿah reported from some Companions of the Prophet ﷺ that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say to the children on the Day of Resurrection, enter Paradise, and they will say, our Lord, shall our fathers and mothers enter? Allāh will say, why do I see them hesitating, enter Paradise, and they will repeat, our Lord, our fathers and mothers, so He will say, enter Paradise you and your parents. > **""Enter Paradise you and your parents.""**"
"18552","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ passed with Companions and a boy on the road, and seeing the company the boy's mother feared trampling, rushing crying, my son, my son, seizing him. The company said, O Messenger of Allāh, she would never cast her son into Fire, so the Prophet ﷺ calmed them saying, no, by Allāh, He casts not His beloved into the Fire. > **""She would never cast her son into Fire, nor does Allāh cast His beloved therein.""**"
"18553","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that captives were brought to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and among them a woman seeking her child, embracing and nursing him. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether she would cast him into Fire, and they said no, so he said Allāh is more merciful to His servants than she to her child. > **""Allāh is more merciful to His servants than this mother to her child.""**"
"18554","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said peoples shall remain in Fire as long as Allāh wills, then Allāh will mercy them and bring them out to Paradise's lowest, washing in a river called Life. Paradise's people will name them the Hell-dwellers; were one to host Paradise's people he would wed, feed, water, and clothe them without diminishing his own. > **""They shall wash in the river of Life and be named the Hell-dwellers.""**"
"18555","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh shall surely glorify Himself on the Day of Resurrection over peoples who never did good at all, bringing them out of the Fire burnt, admitting them to Paradise by His mercy after intercessors' intercession. > **""He shall bring out the burnt who never did good, by mercy after intercession.""**"
"18556","Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said peoples shall emerge from the Fire into Paradise named the Hell-dwellers in Paradise. They will pray Allāh to remove that name, and Allāh will erase it from them, for emerging from the Fire the report was recalled. > **""They shall be named Hell-dwellers in Paradise until Allāh erases that name.""**"
"18557","Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd and ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said when the Day of Resurrection comes and Allāh finishes judging creation, two men will remain and be ordered to the Fire. One will glance back, so the Compeller will say return him, asking why he glanced. He will say he hoped He would admit him to Paradise, so he will be ordered to Paradise, saying Allāh gave him until were he to feed Paradise's people it would diminish nothing with him. > **""He glanced back hoping for Paradise, so he was ordered to Paradise.""**"
"18558","Abū Saʿīd and Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the last to emerge from the Fire are two men. Allāh will say to one, O son of Ādam, what did you prepare for this Day, any good ever or hope in Me? He will say no my Lord, so he will be ordered to the Fire, most remorseful of its people. To the other He will ask the same, and he will say yes my Lord, I hoped if You brought me out You would not return me therein, and he is the last to enter Paradise. > **""The last to emerge are two men, one remorseful to Fire and one hopeful to Paradise.""**"
"18559","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that a servant shall surely call in the Fire a thousand years, O Ḥannān, O Mannān. Allāh will tell Jibrīl, peace be upon him, to bring this servant, and Jibrīl will find Fire's people fallen weeping, return reporting, be told he is in such a place, bring him to stand before his Lord, who will ask how he found his lodging. He will say, my Lord, worst lodging and rest, and He will command return. > **""A servant shall call a thousand years: O Ḥannān, O Mannān.""**"
"18560","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said were you to know Allāh's mercy you would surely rely upon it. > **""Were you to know Allāh's mercy you would surely rely upon it.""**"
"18561","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh created a hundred mercies, one divided among creation, and ninety-nine until the Day of Resurrection. > **""Allāh created a hundred mercies, one divided among creation and ninety-nine reserved.""**"
"18562","Al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī reported that it reached him the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh holds a hundred mercies, dividing one among earth's people so it widened to their terms, storing ninety-nine with Him for His allies on the Day of Resurrection. > **""He divided one mercy to their terms and stored ninety-nine for His allies.""**"
"18563","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ the like thereof. > **""The like thereof concerning the hundred mercies.""**"
"18564","It was also narrated through Jullās the like thereof, briefly. > **""The like thereof through Jullās.""**"
"18565","Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn reported the like thereof. > **""The like thereof from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn.""**"
"18566","Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥaydah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that Allāh created a hundred mercies, one between His creation by which they mercy one another, storing ninety-nine for His allies. > **""One mercy by which creation mercies one another, and ninety-nine for His allies.""**"
"18567","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said our Lord divided His mercy into a hundred parts, sending down one part to earth by which people, birds, and beasts mercy one another, with a hundred mercies remaining with Him but one part for His servants on the Day of Resurrection. > **""He sent down one part by which creatures mercy one another, with ninety-nine for That Day.""**"
